00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:07.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in this program work we look at  childhood obesity and we look at this  important challenge for two major  reasons first childhood obesity is not  just a problem for developed countries  like we tend to believe it is actually a  grave challenge for developing countries  as well and this is because children in  developing countries tend to be both  under nutrition and over nutrition what  it means is that they don't have access  to whole-grain great proteins options  vegetables and fresh fruits but at the  same time they have easy access to fast  food and sugary drink and those options  tend to be cheap and available  everywhere the second reason why we want  to study this is because childhood  obesity actually has a long-term  negative consequence for the children  who are obese when they are young they  tend to be obese as adults as well this  affects their long-term health but also  their psychological health their  performance at school and potentially  their social interactions with others  obese children tend to be bullied at  school or have a harder time making  friends and that could affect their  well-being and their relationship  building and because of all these  reasons we want to study childhood  obesity in this project  I worked in advertising before and I'm  now a faculty specialized in marketing  and so I'm very aware that marketing has  been taken the bad name for facilitating  childhood obesity so we see a lot of  fast food or sugary drinks or these  famous brands using marketing to  influence adult and children's behavior  but what we are trying to do in this  project is to take a novel perspective  and to really think about what marketing  is essentially what market is trying to  do is to communicate certain messages  and to influence people's choice so we  could potentially use marketing for the  greater good of the society  we look at children in elementary school  from the first grade to the sixth grade  so usually that means there are six to  12 years old why we chose the school  it's because school is actually the  environment for these children for the  very first time to make their own choice  at home they may not have a choice of  what they want to eat their parents may  decide for them but in school these  children carry cash with them they get  to actually make a decision of what kind  of lunch they want to eat today or what  kind of snack they want to purchase  today without the immediate influence  from their teachers or their parents so  we believe it was that shaping that  behavior is important because it is a  very autonomous empowering decision for  children when they are choosing their  own food or drink for the first time at  school so this is a great place to  educate them what kind of choices they  should be making that may be better for  them  so in this research program what we did  is we team up with UNICEF so we work for  several years together we launched five  different field experiments in various  schools in elementary schools in Panama  and that allow us to enter the school  and try to communicate what is healthy  choice to children there so what we did  in one program is that we basically use  the print ad concept and we use print  ads to permute to communicate what is  drinking clean water so we educate and  promote clean water to children in  Panama what we found is that by telling  it is basically educating them that  drinking clean water is good for their  health potentially just having trainers  in the school background could be quite  effective in increasing their water  consumption and as a result it can help  to reduce the consumption of sugary  product in another program what we try  to do is to use a very typical marketing  tool price promotion to help promote  healthy choices and this is because we  tend to have the perception that  unhealthy products are cheaper so when  we are trying to save money we may go  with fast food or cheap soda and we tend  to perceive healthy products as pricier  while this might be true in some cases  it is not always true so we try to use  price promotion as a way to provide  another incentive or a motivation for  children to pick healthier snacks at  school so we give them limited time  price promotion and healthy snacks and  we track how much children actually  redeem those coupons to get healthy  snacks and as a result purchased fewer  unhealthy snacks and importantly we  found that price promotions can even be  effective for very young children age of  six to eight even for them they  understand what it means to have limited  time to actually use a coupon they  understand the concept of a good deal  and they may end up choosing or  purchasing a healthy snack at school  because this is our discount right now  and hopefully by trying the healthy  option they gradually build a habit and  preference for healthier options in  general  this line of work can inspire and  motivate scientists from all disciplines  to join us because for me I come from  the background of advertising marketing  and so that is my tool for trying to  shape healthy behavior but we believe  that long term change requires more than  marketing we might be able to have price  promotions in a short run but eventually  if we want to make sure how the options  remain affordable and accessible we  actually need more structural changes on  pricing in the marketplace tax and  government regulation so we actually  need scientists from all different areas  to help us accomplish this and actually  improve children's health in the long  run so I hope that this serves and  inspiration to start a dialogue on what  we can do to help tackle this challenge  [Music]

